        "title": "Urban Transit",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Urban transit is fundamentally a network problem. Its value grows non-linearly with coverage — a transit system that gets you to 90% of destinations is far more than 2× as useful as one covering 45%. This is Metcalfe's law applied to physical mobility. The challenge is that building a high-coverage network costs proportionally to coverage, while the utility benefit grows faster — which creates the political economy of transit: underinvestment is the stable equilibrium unless cities intervene."
    },
